      <section class="capability-opening project-chapter-first" data-scroll-group data-scroll-step="80">
        <h2 id="giving-the-question-answering-assistant-better-knowledge" data-source-anchor="knowledge-management">Keeping the assistant’s knowledge useful</h2>
        <p class="chapter-intro">Researching gaps in the assistant’s knowledge and maintaining the sources behind its answers</p>
        <p>The assistant draws on technical documentation to answer questions, retrieving relevant passages and giving them to a language model as evidence. This approach is known as retrieval-augmented generation, or RAG. Even a well-chosen passage can leave a question unanswered if the documentation is incomplete or out of date. I designed the platform’s Knowledge Management architecture to identify those gaps, research them, and maintain the sources behind the answers.</p>
        <p>A question about an application using an expired certificate illustrates the problem. The library might explain how to renew a certificate without explaining how the application starts using its replacement. Collecting documentation on the right topic would still leave a gap. I needed a way to identify missing knowledge at the level of questions the assistant should be able to answer.</p>
      </section>

      <section data-scroll-group data-scroll-step="80">
        <h3 id="where-the-assistant-needs-more-knowledge">Where does the assistant need more knowledge?</h3>
        <p>Unanswered user questions were a useful starting point, though they could reveal only gaps someone had already encountered. There could also be useful questions nobody had asked yet. I separated two dimensions that were easy to conflate, whether a question had been asked and whether the knowledge base could support an answer.</p>
        <p>A question nobody has asked may already be answerable. Material collected on hosting a Python web app, for example, may also explain how to host a REST API. Both asked and unasked questions therefore need to be checked against the knowledge base, giving four cases with different implications for what to research.</p>
        <p>This distinction gave the acquisition process two inputs, gaps revealed by people and useful gaps predicted by AI. I used AI to propose questions beyond those already raised and check them against the available knowledge. The matrix below shows the four cases and how the two missing cases lead to research. Try predicting a gap, then follow the search for supporting material.</p>
      </section>
      <ItOpsJourney id="certificate-knowledge-journey" class="ops-wide" kind="knowledge" />

      <section data-scroll-group data-scroll-step="80">
        <h3 id="learning-from-the-questions-the-assistant-cannot-answer">Finding the material that will help</h3>
        <p>Knowing which questions needed answers gave us a more useful starting point for acquiring documentation. In the crawler research, a search beginning at a vendor’s documentation homepage could collect many pages while missing the procedure a question required. I used the missing questions to guide AI towards relevant starting pages, then focused the crawl on the material those questions called for.</p>
        <p>Treating every question as a separate search would also miss relationships between the gaps. Certificate expiry, renewal, and a replacement that fails to take effect all concern the certificate lifecycle. I grouped related gaps into a shared research objective, giving the agent a reason to follow useful evidence across documentation sections. The individual questions remained attached to that objective so none disappeared into the broader topic.</p>
        <p>Keeping the questions attached also gave the agent a way to judge its progress. If a question still lacked evidence, it could look for new starting pages. Once the questions were covered and further crawling contributed little useful information, it could send the proposed sources for review. I combined question coverage with information gain so the search could follow relevant discoveries and still recognise when it had enough material. Page and time limits bounded the exploration.</p>
      </section>

      <section data-scroll-group data-scroll-step="80">
        <h3 id="keeping-answers-connected-to-their-sources">What happens when the guidance changes?</h3>
        <p>Once a document had been acquired, its guidance could still change at the original source. By then, the text would have been extracted, split into chunks, and added to the knowledge base. Keeping just those processed passages would leave the system with no reliable place to look for updates. I therefore retained the original source URL during ingestion, making future maintenance part of how knowledge entered the system.</p>
        <p>To understand an update at that URL, the system also needed to know which version it had accepted and which passages came from it. I stored an accepted snapshot alongside the links to its derived knowledge. These records formed the <strong>Source Registry</strong>. They allowed a later check to trace a change in a document back to the knowledge that might need attention.</p>
        <p>Monitoring could then revisit the original URL and compare its main content with the accepted snapshot. Normalising the content before comparison filters out differences in formatting. If the content has changed, AI reads the difference alongside the linked knowledge and assesses its significance. A changed page may contain an updated procedure, or the change may have no bearing on the guidance already held. Detecting the difference and judging its implications require different kinds of work.</p>
        <p>Because the affected knowledge is traceable, the agent can propose replacing or removing specific passages, or request further research if the changed source leaves a question unresolved. Proposed updates go through human review before the ingestion pipeline updates the knowledge and its source records together. The next check then compares against the newly accepted version, preserving the connection between what the assistant knows and the evidence supporting it.</p>
      </section>

