        <h2 id="where-judgement-changes-hands" data-source-anchor="where-judgement-changes-hands">Deciding what to automate</h2>
        <p class="chapter-intro">The roles of AI reasoning, system rules, and human oversight</p>
        <p>These projects required different kinds of judgment. A missing answer called for deciding what to research, while a service ticket called for interpreting the application’s current state. In both cases, I used AI where the next step depended on meaning and context. The surrounding system gave it specific questions to answer, evidence to inspect, and a record of what had already happened.</p>
        <p>The division becomes clearest at a source update or an application change. Comparing two document snapshots can be a repeatable operation, while deciding whether the difference invalidates existing guidance requires interpretation. Sending a browser action is also a defined operation, while deciding that it fulfils the ticket depends on the request and the page. Human review provides the authority to approve consequential changes, with the proposed work and its supporting evidence available for inspection.</p>
        <p>I want AI to take on more of the investigation and execution involved in IT support while making that work easier for people to follow. That depends on architecture as much as model capability. The source records, observation loop, and points of intervention determine whether a person can understand a proposal, correct it, and trust the system to continue from that correction.</p>
